---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import PageNext from '../../components/PageNext.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import CapabilityMatrix from '../../components/CapabilityMatrix.astro';
import { DOCTOR_GATES } from '../../data/stats';
---

<Layout title="Máddu vs Paperclip" pathname="/compare/paperclip">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow-chain">
        <span class="eyebrow">Compare</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">agent control planes</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">head-on</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Máddu vs Paperclip. <span class="dim-h1">The record is the difference.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Paperclip is the closest thing to a direct competitor Máddu has — and a good tool. Both do locks, budgets, and approvals. The split is <em>where the truth lives</em>: their control plane, or your repo.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 01 · What Paperclip is</span>
        <h2>An org chart for agent teams.</h2>
        <p>
          Paperclip is an open-source orchestration platform for managing teams of AI agents: org-chart roles and reporting lines, atomic task checkout with execution locks, per-agent budgets, heartbeat scheduling, and approval workflows. It drives Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini, and bash-based agents. At roughly 70k+ GitHub stars as of July 2026, its ecosystem is orders of magnitude larger than Máddu's.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="vs-honest">
        <h4>Where Paperclip is stronger — plainly</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Ecosystem and community size — ~1000× Máddu's, with the contributor depth that brings.</li>
          <li>Organizational modeling — roles, reporting lines, and permissions as first-class visual structure.</li>
          <li>Scheduling — heartbeat-driven agent teams out of the box.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 02 · Where Máddu differs</span>
        <h2>Their locks manage the work. The spine survives it.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Repo-owned record</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">tamper-evident</span>
          </div>
          <p>Máddu's governance history is a hash-chained, append-only spine in <code>.maddu/</code> — it travels with <code>git clone</code>, survives the platform, and verifies with <code>maddu spine verify</code>. A control plane's history lives in the control plane.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Deterministic gates</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">{DOCTOR_GATES} + ci</span>
          </div>
          <p>Locks and budgets bound what agents <em>may</em> do. Máddu also checks what they <em>did</em>: {DOCTOR_GATES} deterministic gates, a headless <code>maddu ci</code> rail, and a completion-claim check that demands observed proof for every "done".</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Trust that's earned</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">recommend-only</span>
          </div>
          <p>Per-lane autonomy is scored from verified slice outcomes and only ever <em>recommended</em> — the operator applies it. Permissions you assign are a policy; trust computed from the record is evidence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="warn" title="Same wedge, opposite center of gravity" tag="the real split">
        <p>
          If you want a mission-control platform that runs your agent org, Paperclip is built for that. If you want the governance record to belong to the repository — auditable with <code>cat</code>, alive after any tool dies — that is the entire reason Máddu exists.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <CapabilityMatrix
        players={['paperclip']}
        eyebrow="§ 03 · The matrix"
        title="Axis by axis."
        subtitle="The fifteen research axes, focused on this matchup. Conservative marks — shipped, documented surfaces only."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 04 · Choose honestly</span>
        <h2>Two defensible choices.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Choose Paperclip when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>You're running a standing team of agents with roles and schedules</li>
            <li>Visual org management matters more than a portable audit trail</li>
            <li>You want the largest possible community around the tool</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Choose Máddu when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>The audit record must live in the repo and survive every tool swap</li>
            <li>You want deterministic verification, not just permission boundaries</li>
            <li>Zero infrastructure is a requirement, not a preference</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="feat-note">
        Related: <a href="/features/worktrees">worktree lanes</a> · <a href="/features/autonomy">earned autonomy</a> · <a href="/features/ci">governance in CI</a> · <a href="/hard-rules">the hard rules</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PageNext
    eyebrow="Next comparison"
    title="Máddu vs the worktree orchestrators."
    hint="Vibe Kanban, Claude Squad, oh-my-claudecode, Ruflo, kodo — session managers vs a governance record."
    href="/compare/orchestrators"
    tone="accent"
  />
  <CtaBlock />
</Layout>

<style>
  .vs-honest {
    padding: var(--m-space-4) var(--m-space-5);
    background: var(--m-bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--m-line);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--m-warn);
    border-radius: var(--m-radius);
    max-width: 720px;
  }
  .vs-honest h4 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--m-space-2);
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--m-warn);
  }
  .vs-honest ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
  .vs-honest li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); }
  .vs-list { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  .vs-list li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); line-height: 1.5; }
  .feat-note {
    margin-top: var(--m-space-5);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--m-fg-2);
  }
  .feat-note code { font-size: 12px; }
</style>
